@startuml
title Zama Protocol — the six key artifacts and where each one lives
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam componentBackgroundColor #F3EEFB
skinparam componentBorderColor #7A5BB5
skinparam packageBackgroundColor #FCFAFE
skinparam packageBorderColor #7A5BB5
skinparam ArrowColor #7A5BB5
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #F9F6FD
skinparam noteBorderColor #7A5BB5

package "Generated once per key id by the KMS" as GEN {
  component "TFHE public key\nencrypts every input,\nfor every user and contract" as PK
  component "Evaluation key\nbootstrapping and\nkey-switching material" as EK
  component "Secret key shares\nthreshold-split, never\nreassembled anywhere" as SK #FDEEEE
  component "ZKPoK CRS\none per bit capacity" as CRS
}

package "Client, per account" as CLI {
  component "secp256k1 account key\nsigns transactions and the\ndecryption authorisation" as ACC
  component "ML-KEM-512 keypair\nseals one decryption\nresult in transit" as MLKEM
}

package "On-chain" as CHAIN {
  component "ACL\nwhich address may read\nwhich handle" as ACL
  component "KMSVerifier\nauthorised signer set\nand threshold" as VER
}

PK --> CLI : downloaded and cached
CRS --> CLI : downloaded and cached
EK --> [Coprocessors] : compute on ciphertexts
SK --> [KMS nodes] : one share each
ACC --> ACL : identity the ACL grants to
MLKEM --> [KMS nodes] : public half travels\nwith the request
[KMS nodes] --> VER : signer set published on-chain

note right of SK
  This is the only key that can turn a
  ciphertext into a plaintext, and no
  single party ever holds it.
end note

note right of MLKEM
  Not the key protecting the balance.
  It protects the answer on the way back.
end note
@enduml
